WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.240
FOMO:
Everysince ChatGPT came out, the tech landscape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:09.420
hasn’t been the same. As if suddenly hit by a&nbsp;
sudden burst of enthusiasm, or FOMO, the titans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:14.460
of the tech world have rapidly assembled massive&nbsp;
AI departments and begun working. Facebook,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:20.460
Amazon, Apple, Google and basically everyone&nbsp;
else has embarked on a race to finally outdo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:27.240
one another and come out as the supreme victor.&nbsp;
Their goal? Simple, create the next big thing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:33.180
The next YouTube, the next Google, the next&nbsp;
Instagram. They have funnelled an unprecedented&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:38.820
amount of resources in research, laboratories,&nbsp;
compensation, marketing, hardware, software,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:44.460
and everything else required to bring these&nbsp;
remarkable innovations to market. The promise of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:50.460
AI's potential was simply too enticing to ignore:&nbsp;
self-evolving algorithms, human like machines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:57.600
unbelievable automation, and most importantly&nbsp;
unthinkable riches and control. But, the reality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:05.940
is that FOMO has blinded these giants to a simple&nbsp;
yet glaring truth. They have already lost AI, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:11.220
pouring hundreds of billions into a lost battle&nbsp;
isn’t going to change anything. Let me explain.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:15.480
PAST PERFORMANCE:&nbsp;

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:26.460
In the stock market, there’s a famous saying&nbsp;
“Past performance is not an indicator of future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:31.800
results.” Aka, just because a stock has been&nbsp;
performing well does not mean that it will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:37.320
continue to perform well. Most investors say&nbsp;
this tongue in cheek. They know that it’s true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:41.940
but they continue to invest in companies and&nbsp;
funds largely because they have performed well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:47.640
in the past. Companies are even more susceptible&nbsp;
to this logical fallacy as they have been directly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:52.320
rewarded for such behavior in the past. The&nbsp;
only reason that they got so big in the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:57.900
place is because they gambled big on their hero&nbsp;
products whether that’s the iPhone or Google or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:02.700
WhatsApp. They went against the grain and bet&nbsp;
it all. Remember how much criticism Facebook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:08.520
received for spending $19 billion on WhatsApp&nbsp;
back in 2014? This gamble ended up paying off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:14.340
but let’s not forget what it was: a gamble. Each&nbsp;
time, these companies win a gamble, they’re more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:18.720
inclined to think that they can dominate any&nbsp;
industry they want if they just spend enough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:24.960
money. But this notion is fundamentally flawed&nbsp;
and you don’t have to look much further than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:30.000
Meta. After making deca billion dollar bets with&nbsp;
WhatsApp and Instagram and seeing them pay off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.000
Zuckerberg felt confident to start betting in&nbsp;
the hundreds of billions with the Metaverse and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:41.280
think we all remember how that turned out. But the&nbsp;
Metaverse is merely their most publicized failure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:46.620
Meta’s list of failures is far larger than&nbsp;
that. Take Poke for example. In the early 2010s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:51.720
after seeing how well SnapChat was doing, and&nbsp;
failing to acquire Snapchat, Facebook decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:57.360
to launch a clone called Poke. The premise seemed&nbsp;
straightforward: leveraging their vast user base&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:02.340
and existing infrastructure to launch a rival app&nbsp;
which should’ve been a slamdunk against SnapChat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:07.560
But, Poke would end up flopping so hard that&nbsp;
Facebook would end up deleting Poke from the app&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:12.480
store without even publicly mentioning it. Jump&nbsp;
forward a few years and Zuckerberg would start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:18.240
betting on space, the apparent next big thing.&nbsp;
He was gonna build this grand drone internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:24.300
until he quietly shut that down as well. The&nbsp;
Metaverse gamble followed, as did bets on AI and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:29.700
most recently, the Threads endeavor. I mean, with&nbsp;
Threads, their daily active users was literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:36.000
down over 80% within just a month of launch. With&nbsp;
each and every one of these products, Zuckerberg&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:41.100
was trying to create the next big thing. The next&nbsp;
big social media, the next big space project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:48.120
the next big VR project, and the next big AI&nbsp;
project but each and evertime, they miserably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:53.520
failed. This isn’t to say that the sector itself&nbsp;
failed though. SnapChat has 400 million users.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:04:00.060
Twitter has 370 million users. Starlink is doing&nbsp;
great. And someone will eventually win VR. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:05.640
simply that that someone wasn’t Facebook. The&nbsp;
reality is that Facebook has only launched 1&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:10.260
next big thing product in their entire history&nbsp;
and that was their original product: Facebook.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:16.080
They had 2 successful acquistions: Instagram and&nbsp;
WhatsApp, but that pretty much sums up the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:21.960
company. Everything else they tried was a complete&nbsp;
failure. This isn’t a jab against Facebook as it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.960 --> 00:04:27.600
true with all of their peers as well. When was the&nbsp;
last time that Google or Apple or Amazon actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:31.980
launched something that was even comparable&nbsp;
to their hero product? Google has arguably had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:37.320
the most number of successful products but at&nbsp;
this point, it’s been over a decade since they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:42.540
launched a winner as well. The reason is obvious,&nbsp;
it’s simply ridiculously hard to replicate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:46.980
success of something like Facebook or YouTube&nbsp;
even for companies that have already done it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:52.620
once or twice. This brings us back to the original&nbsp;
statement: “Past performance is not an indicator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:57.720
of future results.” Just because Facebook launched&nbsp;
a successful social media 20 years ago and Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:02.580
launched a successful search engine 25 years ago&nbsp;
doesn’t mean that they have a better chance of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:08.760
winning AI today. In fact, I would actually argue&nbsp;
that it leaves them worse of. Let me explain.

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:13.800
BAGGAGE:&nbsp;

00:05:13.800 --> 00:05:24.180
Everyone is so quick to point out all of the&nbsp;
advantages enjoyed by these tech giants. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:29.100
command the finest engineers, possess unparalleled&nbsp;
infrastructure, possess seemingly limitless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:34.680
capital, and boast extensive reach, among other&nbsp;
assets. But rarely do people consider these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:41.640
companies’ biggest shortfall: an incomprehensible&nbsp;
amount of baggage. Aside from Wall Street and big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:46.860
pharma, big tech is likely the most hated&nbsp;
industry in the world. People see them as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:52.440
monopolies with way too much power, control, and&nbsp;
influence. Not to mention the privacy concerns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:57.660
Only a vocal minority voice their concerns about&nbsp;
these companies or go as far as ditching WhatsApp&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:03.540
or Gmail. But that isn’t to say that everyone else&nbsp;
is happy with these platforms. And likely the best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:09.240
example of this is with YouTube creators. When a&nbsp;
YouTuber starts using too much clickbait or too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:14.700
many thirst traps, only a vocal minority will call&nbsp;
them out. Everyone else will just be less inclined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.700 --> 00:06:19.560
to watch their next video. When you compound this&nbsp;
over months and years, that’s how you end up with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.560 --> 00:06:24.420
a dead channel. It’s the same thing with big&nbsp;
tech. People aren’t going to ditch Gmail just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.420 --> 00:06:28.320
because Google is notorious for collecting&nbsp;
data or implementing anti consumer friendly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:33.660
policies. But when they eventually get the chance&nbsp;
to choose between Bard and ChatGPT, they’re gonna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:39.120
choose ChatGPT everyday of the week. In fact, I&nbsp;
would argue that this bias is the number 1 reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:44.700
that otherwise great products don’t take off. Take&nbsp;
Threads for example. One of the biggest critiques&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:50.220
of Threads is that it’s just a ripoff of Twitter&nbsp;
but that’s quite a funny argument because that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.220 --> 00:06:55.380
was never a showstopper before. Facebook itself&nbsp;
wasn't the pioneer in the realm of social media.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.380 --> 00:07:00.420
Does Friendster and MySpace ring a bell? And&nbsp;
what about Google, is there a single thing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:06.060
they’ve launched that’s not a rip off? They didn’t&nbsp;
create search engines or email or video sharing or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:12.420
browsers or smartphones or really anything. They&nbsp;
just made it better and that was enough. Recent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:16.860
products like Threads and Bard, however, appear&nbsp;
to be judged more by their perceived lack of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:23.940
originality than by their actual merits. This&nbsp;
phenomenon is more about optics than product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:29.760
authenticity. Imagine if Threads was a startup.&nbsp;
If Threads was a startup, the optics would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:34.980
completely different. Threads would’ve been seen&nbsp;
as the valiant underdog taking on the once great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:40.380
Twitter that was ruined by Elon Musk. But, since&nbsp;
Threads is from Facebook, people perceive it as:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:46.140
Oh great, greedy Facebook is trying to expand&nbsp;
their monopoly to yet another sector. Woohoo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.140 --> 00:07:51.240
You could almost see this playing out real time.&nbsp;
After Threads launched, everyone checked it out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:56.580
just to see what all the hype was about but no one&nbsp;
really resonated with the product or story. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.580 --> 00:08:02.640
this is why it’s so dangerous for these big tech&nbsp;
companies to enter the AI scene. More than likely,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:07.860
they’re just gonna burn extraordinary amounts&nbsp;
of money and end up losing anyway. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.860 --> 00:08:13.680
this trend has already begun with Bard. The&nbsp;
common sentiment around Bard is that ChatGPT&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:20.520
was the OG revolutionary product and that Bard&nbsp;
just copy pasted ChatGPT but this is completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.520 --> 00:08:27.120
false. Google has been working on AI for longer&nbsp;
than OpenAI’s entire existence. Now, you might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:33.660
be saying, “Oh well, Google probably wasn’t as&nbsp;
focused as OpenAI”, but this is also completely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.660 --> 00:08:40.920
false. In total, OpenAI has raised $11.3 billion&nbsp;
which is an insane amount for a startup. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:46.560
do you wanna guess how much Google has invested&nbsp;
into AI? Maybe $20 billion? $40 billion? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:53.580
it’s actually upwards of $200 billion. Google has&nbsp;
literally been investing OpenAI’s entire funding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:58.980
into AI every 6 months for the past decade to&nbsp;
come up with Bard. But, unfortunately for Google,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:04.620
it looks like Bard is gonna end up as the next&nbsp;
Bing while ChatGPT ends up as the next Google.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:11.340
The bottomline is that people have already made&nbsp;
up their minds about big tech. So, outspending the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:16.740
competition isn’t gonna to allow these companies&nbsp;
come out on top. But then, what’s the solution?

00:09:16.740 --> 00:09:19.980
DIVERSIFICATION VS CONCENTRATION:&nbsp;

00:09:19.980 --> 00:09:31.620
Well, the solution is easy, FAANG must comes&nbsp;
to terms with reality. The reality that AI will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:37.020
only yield a limited number of true winners, and&nbsp;
statistically speaking, it's highly improbable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.020 --> 00:09:42.900
that the big tech giants will emerge as these&nbsp;
winners despite their vast resources. Combine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:48.720
this with overwhelming negative public sentiment&nbsp;
against these companies and it becomes damn near&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.720 --> 00:09:55.500
impossible for FAANG to “win” AI. So, should they&nbsp;
just sit back and watch as their fortunes crumble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.500 --> 00:10:00.300
thanks to up and coming AI products? Well of&nbsp;
course not, that would be braindead behavior.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.840 --> 00:10:06.960
But, if sitting back is a bad idea and going all&nbsp;
in is also a bad idea, what in the world are they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:12.720
supposed to do? Well, easy. Instead trying to&nbsp;
strike gold themselves, they should be selling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:18.720
the shovels. This is exactly what Nvidia has&nbsp;
been doing and I mean, the results speak for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:24.600
themselves. Nvidia was originally a consumer&nbsp;
brand focused on selling GPUs to gamers. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:29.400
screwed up their PR with gamers when they started&nbsp;
embracing cryptocurrency miners though. Gamers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.400 --> 00:10:34.200
felt like Nvidia betrayed them and that Nvidia&nbsp;
was just milking gamers and yadayadayada.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:40.860
When AI starting gaining steam, Nvidia could’ve&nbsp;
easily tried to make their own inhouse consumer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.860 --> 00:10:47.040
AI platform. They had all the tech and money to&nbsp;
do so. But instead, they chose to bet on something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:54.240
far more consistent and reliable: selling shovels.&nbsp;
Now, it doesn’t matter to them who wins the AI&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.240 --> 00:10:59.520
game. It could be Apple or Google or Facebook or&nbsp;
OpenAI or some startup no ones has ever heard of.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:07.380
It doesn’t matter because they’re all using Nvidia&nbsp;
GPUs. In this sense, Nvidia has already won AI and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.380 --> 00:11:11.760
secured their future within the new era without&nbsp;
actually having to take the massive gamble of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:17.940
creating the next big thing. The same thing can&nbsp;
be said about Microsoft. About 10 years ago, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:22.920
decided that they were gonna be a backend company&nbsp;
focused on supporting great solutions instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:30.420
creating great solutions. The result? After 17&nbsp;
years of stagnation, Microsoft would nearly 7X&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.420 --> 00:11:36.120
within 4 years. Now of course, supporting these&nbsp;
solutions wont be as lucrative as creating them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.120 --> 00:11:42.120
but the probability of success is also more like&nbsp;
90% as opposed to less than 1 in a million. Also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:48.180
for the record, supporting AI is already insanely&nbsp;
lucrative. Google seems to be recognizing this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:52.200
paradigm shift, as they’ve starting focusing&nbsp;
more on expanding their cloud business rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.200 --> 00:11:56.760
than solely launching consumer-centric products.&nbsp;
Facebook on the other hand though is still going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.760 --> 00:12:02.700
all out on gambles whether that’s the Metaverse,&nbsp;
AI, or Threads. And it’s the same case with many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.700 --> 00:12:08.760
of the other companies at the top as they’ve&nbsp;
all been blinded by FOMO. And all there gonna&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:13.620
have to show for it in 5 or 10 years is a slew&nbsp;
of undesired products and hundreds of billions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:19.200
of dollars less in the bank. The problem is that&nbsp;
much of big tech is trying to buy naked calls on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:24.720
AI when the smarter solution is to just sell&nbsp;
puts, by offering the foundational tools and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:31.260
support necessary for the AI landscape to flourish&nbsp;
and laugh all the way to the bank. But that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:35.940
just what I think. If you’d like to learn about&nbsp;
how AI got to this point, check out this video.

